[interviewer · CLARIFY · 2:35] I don't have competitor benchmark data. Focus on Brew & Bean's own economics.

Go ahead and lay out your structure.

[candidate · 4:05] Okay. Since revenue is growing but margin collapsed from 24% to 6%, this is almost certainly a cost problem, so I'd weight my structure there.

Profit = Revenue minus Costs. On the revenue side, I'd break the 15% growth into price versus volume, because if they grew revenue purely by discounting and driving traffic, revenue can rise while gross margin falls. So: price per transaction, number of transactions, and mix across drinks versus food versus retail bags.

On the cost side, I'd split fixed and variable. Fixed: rent, base labor, equipment, corporate overhead. Variable: cost of goods — beans, milk, cups — plus hourly labor that flexes with volume, and delivery-app commissions if they use them.

Then I'd compare each line as a percent of revenue in year zero versus today, and find which lines expanded. An 18-point margin swing is huge, so it should be one or two lines, not diffuse.

Can I get the cost structure as a percent of revenue for both years?
