[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 11:34] I don't have price elasticity or pricing-test data.

Set the recommendation aside for a moment. Beyond raising prices, what other levers could the client pull to reverse this? Give me the full set.

[candidate · 13:04] A few beyond price. First, procurement: with 200 stores they have real scale, so I'd look at long-term supplier contracts or hedging on beans and dairy rather than buying at spot — that's how they got fully exposed to a 40% swing. Consolidating vendors across the network should also buy volume discounts.

Second, product mix: steer customers toward higher-margin items. Black coffee and espresso have better margins than elaborate dairy-heavy drinks; loyalty promotions and menu placement can shift mix without a visible price change.

Third, portion and waste control: standardize shot and milk pours, track brewed coffee dumped at close. In a business where COGS is 58% of revenue, a few points of waste is real money.

Fourth, indirect pricing — shrink portion sizes slightly, or charge for what used to be free, like alternative milks. Customers notice that less than headline price changes.

Fifth, reformulate: adjust the blend toward less expensive origins if quality holds.

Overhead drifted 12 to 14 points too — smaller, but worth a look.
