[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 14:48] Now bring it together. Before we wrap, on what you asked about earlier: The average transaction is $6.80, up from $6.20 two years ago — about a 10% increase, because customers are buying more items per visit. 200 stores today — and it was 200 stores two years ago, so the footprint hasn’t changed. What's your recommendation to the CEO?

[candidate · 20:10] Quick note on that: ticket rose 10% to $6.80 but menu prices are flat, so that's customers adding items — and with revenue up 15%, transaction count is only up about 5% ($1.15 / $1.10). So traffic is nearly flat; growth came from upselling into items that are apparently dragging margin down.

One clarification before I commit: is the CEO constrained on price for brand reasons — has leadership already ruled a price increase off the table? That changes how hard I lean on it.

Assuming not, my recommendation: raise menu prices roughly 10-12% in staged increments, starting with a pilot in a subset of stores to read elasticity.

Why: all 18 points of margin loss trace to input inflation — beans up 40%, other inputs up 37.5% — absorbed with zero pass-through over two years. Nothing else moved; labor is flat.

Math: a 10% price increase on flat volume adds ~10 points of margin, taking 6% to roughly 15-16%.

Support it with supplier contracts or hedging on beans and dairy, portion and waste standards, and a mix review once we have gross profit dollars by item.

Risks: elasticity is unknown, and traffic is already soft at +5%. Hence the pilot.

Next steps: two-week price test in 20 stores, item-level margin data, and a procurement review.
